    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the fluid looks pretty clean though, no burnt smell or anything. i'm leaning towards it being a mechanical issue in the valve body or maybe a bad solenoid. if i pull the pan, is there anything specific in the valve body or shifting logic that would cause those spikes to be so erratic rather than just a constant low pressure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When examining the valve body, you should check the flapper positions, cartridges, and shift solenoids for malfunction. Damaged or worn valves can result in erratic pressure changes due to inconsistent opening and closing, rather than maintaining a steady pressure. A faulty pressure control solenoid would also cause inappropriate hydraulic power distribution, leading to spikes. Furthermore, bad tensioners in the tensioner pulley area can intermittently affect the pressure as the constant release and reapplying of pressure occur. Thoroughly inspecting each of these components will narrow down the cause of your erratic pressure readings.",
